<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Karelians</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1278 CE (approx.) · capital Korela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Finnic people around Lake Ladoga and their stronghold of Korela, allies of Novgorod whose land was divided between Sweden and Rus' in 1323.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Karelians emerged in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> on the north-western shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a> and the Karelian Isthmus. Their wealth came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, and by the 12th century they were closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a>, joining its campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> and Swedes.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Karelian society was led by local chiefs and wealthy families who controlled fur collection from the north. Rich graves with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ornaments and swords show a prosperous elite. In 1227 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a> had many Karelians baptised into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a>, and Korela became their main fortified centre.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1278 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a> campaigned against the Karelians and tightened its control over them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> pressed from the west and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a> in 1293. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-noteborg">Treaty of Nöteborg</a> in 1323 divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> between Sweden and Novgorod, a border that shaped the later divide between Lutheran <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karelians.jpg" alt="Karelians" width="320" height="179"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Boris23 assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:It%C3%A4karjalaisten_lippu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1278 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Korela</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Finnic paganism, later Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Karelian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia, Finland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karelia, Korela</dd></dl></aside></div>
